[{:seon.task/name "with-merge-mg-check-cost"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.check/mg-check` prints each checked contract's mg/check ms (100 runs, seed 42). Measured (load about 10): of the 82 contracts' ~130 s, `seon.template/with-merge` alone takes 57 s (570 ms a run), then `seon.task/cycle-in` 15 s, `seon.proofs/failures` 11.7 s and `seon.proofs/costs` 11.2 s; the median contract is well under 1 s, so with-merge sets the floor of a sharded mg/check (4 shards: 94 s wall). with-merge is one pass over its declarations, so the cost is in generating or checking `[:vector :seon.task/declaration]` (the generator's size, the instrumented contract validating every nested declaration in and out, or `seon.task/task-id` and `seon.task/default-branch`'s own contracts per element). Find which by measuring each part (generate only, validate only, call uninstrumented vs instrumented) and fix it at its cause: a schema whose generator is too large gets a bounded :gen; a contract check that repeats work does it once. Proof: a `seon.cost` proof that with-merge plus its contract check grows linearly in the declarations and costs no more per declaration than one task-id and one default-branch call (from `seon.cost` units), at three sizes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.template-test/with-merge-checks-in-its-share}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-request-is-one-linked-transaction"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists (merged; DECISIONS: the owner's 2026-10-22 answers on requests, failed tasks and task content; notes/archive/DESIGN-tasks.md D2(a), D3(a), D4, D5(a)):\n- `my.root/request!` makes a request (ns, name, plan, optional proofs and model) one task that designs, builds and merges itself (`seon.template/whole`, the default); for an experiment it takes `seon.template/design-build` (its design-build task, kind :work, followed by its merge task on its branch, linked by :needs: `seon.template/with-merge`) or `seon.template/stages` (design -> implement -> merge on design's branch) in its place. `my.root/request!` writes `seon.task/create-tx` and `seon.task/execution-tx` (`seon.template/request-tx`) in one `seon.root/transact-main!`, and the reconciler forks only from a committed main, so every agent of the set starts from the same graph.\n- The task rides the writer's :spawn and fork messages to `seon.agent/init!`; `seon.turn/current-task` reads it (`[:turn :task]` deleted), so `my.task/current` is known from an agent's first read.\n- An unrun proof refuses done as :proof-unknown, not :proof-red (`seon.reconcile/done-explain`), as v1 counted it.\n- A failed task is held for root: `my.task/fail!` writes `:seon.task/failure` {:reason :counterexample :run} on the task on its branch and ends the loop (outcome :failed); the judge carries this run's failure onto main with the task held; `seon.reconcile/task-state` derives :failed; `seon.problems/report` has a :failed class; a release (`my.root/run!`) is root's answer. Deleted: `my.task/send-back!` (a turn stop the judge refined into an automatic retry).\n- Task content reaches main at the run's end: `seon.task/carry-tx` compares base, branch and main over `seon.merge/carried-platform-attrs`; a branch-only change overwrites main's, and a two-sided different edit is recorded on the run (`:seon.run/task-conflicts`, the :task-conflict class of `seon.problems/report`) for root. The writer's :branch-db read answers the merge-base db. Deleted: `seon.task/plan-tx`.\nOpen (root's), for the staged experiment: proofs still accumulate along :needs, and a design step's new proofs reach main only as the plan's text, so an implement step whose request carried no proofs is refused :no-proofs by root's judge."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.template-test/a-design-build-request-is-its-design-build-task-and-its-merge
                      seon.template-test/the-stages-are-design-implement-merge-on-one-branch
                      seon.template-test/a-request-is-one-transaction-and-forks-after-it
                      seon.reconcile-test/done-explain-table
                      seon.agent-test/a-forked-agent-knows-its-task
                      seon.reconcile-test/a-task-marked-failed-is-held-for-root
                      seon.agent-test/a-failure-marked-through-the-server-holds-the-task
                      seon.reconcile-test/a-branch-carries-every-task-it-changed-and-records-a-two-sided-edit}}
 {:seon.task/name "templates-are-held-task-data"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: the templates are strings in `seon.template/instructions`, keyed by `:seon.task/kind`; `seon.template/whole` copies :whole's, `seon.template/design-build` :work's into its design-build task and `with-merge` :merge's into its merge, and `seon.template/stages` copies :design's and :implement's into its stages; `seon.task/accumulate` hard-codes which members flow, and proofs accumulate along :needs (docs/tasks.md#a-request-is-one-transaction says this is unsettled). Owner, 2026-10-22 (DECISIONS): templates are held tasks in resources/seon/tasks, loaded by `my.root/load-tasks!`; `:seon.task/owned` on each member's schema says what carries; proofs are owned, not carried; design writing implement's proofs is seon.task/design-writes-the-implement-steps-proofs; `:seon.task/kind` goes, and the attributes present decide (a merge carries :seon.task/target). Deletes: `seon.template/instructions`, the join in `seon.task/accumulate`, :seon.task/kind."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.task/design-writes-the-implement-steps-proofs"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.template-test/a-template-is-a-held-task seon.task-test/owned-members-carry-by-schema-property seon.task-test/proofs-never-accumulate}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-design-writes-its-proof-first"
  :seon.task/plan "Pipeline 1 (notes/live-2026-10-25.md F4): the two printer designs reproduced their bug by turn 11 (`:id` under the `#` of `#:seon.task{`; a keyword past a cut), then read seon.print one function a turn to the cap and registered no proof; one wrote the fix's body, which the design leaves to implement. The design instructions (```seon.template/instructions```) said to read what the plan touches and try the idea, then write the proofs: reading came first, with no end. Now they say proof first: reproduce what the plan says is wrong as a deftest in its test namespace, register it, run it red, then read what the change touches. Second cause, from the same runs: a guessed name's source pointed at `(source ns)`, which answered the ns form alone; the agent took it for all that was recorded and spent five turns finding the namespace's private functions. ```my.agent/source-fn``` of a namespace now answers its ns form, then every definition a line (```seon.docs/definition-lines``` of ```seon.ref/namespace-view```, the lines a namespace's citation shows), and ```my.agent/not-found``` points there. Replaces the ns-form-only answer; no new mechanism."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.template-test/each-steps-instructions-are-its-task-briefly-prose-first seon.repl-test/an-agent-repl-answers-what-a-clojure-repl-answers}}]
